---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import CTASection from '../../components/CTASection.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Choosing a Home Improvement Contractor in Kissimmee | Guide"
  description="Owner-operated vs. franchise contractors in Central Florida. Questions to ask, red flags to avoid, and what matters when hiring for your Kissimmee home."
  ogImage="/images/crew-branded-shirt.jpg"
  schema={{
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@graph": [
      {
        "@type": "Article",
        "@id": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides/choosing-a-home-improvement-contractor-kissimmee/#article",
        "headline": "Choosing a Home Improvement Contractor in Kissimmee",
        "description": "Guide to hiring contractors in Central Florida. Owner-operated vs franchise, questions to ask, and red flags.",
        "author": {
          "@type": "Organization",
          "name": "GO PRO HOME IMPROVEMENTS INC."
        },
        "publisher": {
          "@type": "Organization",
          "name": "GO PRO HOME IMPROVEMENTS INC.",
          "logo": {
            "@type": "ImageObject",
            "url": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/images/logo.png"
          }
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
        "@id": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides/choosing-a-home-improvement-contractor-kissimmee/#breadcrumbs",
        "itemListElement": [
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 1,
            "name": "Home",
            "item": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com"
          },
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 2,
            "name": "Guides",
            "item": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides"
          },
          {
            "@type": "ListItem",
            "position": 3,
            "name": "Choosing a Contractor",
            "item": "https://goprohomeimprovements.com/guides/choosing-a-home-improvement-contractor-kissimmee"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }}
>
  <Header />

  <section class="bg-gopro-charcoal text-white py-16 border-b-4 border-gopro-orange">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <nav class="text-sm mb-6 font-sans">
        <a href="/" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Home</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <a href="/guides" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-gopro-orange transition-colors">Guides</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-gray-500">/</span>
        <span class="text-white">Choosing a Contractor</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-4 tracking-tight">Choosing a Home Improvement Contractor in Kissimmee</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-3xl font-sans">
        Owner-operated vs. franchise. Questions to ask, red flags to avoid, and what actually matters when hiring for your home.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-padding">
    <div class="container-custom">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
        <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">
          <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-6 font-sans">
            Hiring a contractor in <a href="/areas/kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Kissimmee</a>, <a href="/areas/orlando" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Orlando</a>, or Central Florida means choosing between owner-operated companies (like us) and franchise operations. Here's what to ask, what to watch for, and what actually matters when someone's working on your home.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Owner-Operated vs. Franchise</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Owner-Operated Contractors</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Small businesses where the owner is on-site or directly managing every job. Crews are smaller, pricing is straightforward, and the person who gave you the estimate is accountable if something goes wrong. Most owner-operated contractors in Central Florida specialize in 2-4 services (<a href="/services/interior-exterior-painting" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">painting</a>, <a href="/services/drywall" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">drywall</a>, <a href="/services/trim-crown-molding" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">trim</a>, etc.) and have done hundreds of similar jobs.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Pros:</strong> Personal accountability, direct communication, often lower overhead (no franchise fees), owner reputation is on the line every job.<br />
            <strong>Cons:</strong> Smaller crew availability (can't start 10 jobs at once), less brand recognition than national franchises.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Franchise Contractors</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            National brands with local franchisees. Bigger crews, more marketing, and standardized processes. The person who shows up may not be the owner, and quality can vary between franchise locations even under the same brand.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Pros:</strong> Brand recognition, bigger crews (can start faster), national warranty programs.<br />
            <strong>Cons:</strong> Higher overhead (franchise fees, national ad campaigns) gets passed to you, less personal accountability, crew quality varies.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gopro-orange/10 border-l-4 border-gopro-orange p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-0">
              <strong>Our take:</strong> Owner-operated contractors live and die on reputation. Franchises rely on the brand. If you value personal accountability and direct communication, go local. If you want a big-name brand and don't mind paying for it, franchises work fine.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Questions to Ask Before Hiring</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">1. How Long Have You Been in Business?</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Longevity matters. A contractor who's been around 10+ years has survived slow markets, earned repeat business, and knows how to run jobs properly. New contractors aren't automatically bad, but experience counts.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Red flag:</strong> Can't or won't answer. Vague answers like "been doing this forever" without specifics.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">2. Are You Insured?</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            General liability insurance protects you if the contractor damages your property or someone gets hurt on the job. Any legitimate contractor carries it. Ask for proof—a certificate of insurance with current dates.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Red flag:</strong> "Don't worry, I'm covered" without showing proof. Expired insurance. No insurance at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">3. Can You Provide References or Examples of Past Work?</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Most contractors have photos of recent jobs or can connect you with past clients. Check <a href="/reviews" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">online reviews</a>, ask for references, or look at their social media (Instagram, Facebook). If they've been in business for years but have zero proof of past work, that's odd.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Red flag:</strong> No photos, no reviews, no references. "All my work is word-of-mouth" sounds good but makes verification impossible.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">4. What's Included in Your Estimate?</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            A real estimate lists labor, materials, prep work, number of coats, cleanup, and timeline. If the estimate just says "paint house $3,500," you can't compare it to other bids because you don't know what's included.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Red flag:</strong> Vague scope. No written estimate. Pressure to sign same-day without details.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">5. Who Will Actually Do the Work?</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Some contractors subcontract everything. That's not automatically bad, but you should know. If the owner gives the estimate but hires day laborers to do the job, quality and accountability drop.
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Red flag:</strong> Can't or won't say who's doing the work. "I'll figure it out when we start."
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">6. What Happens If I'm Not Happy?</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Good contractors fix problems. Ask what their process is if you're not satisfied. Do they come back and make it right, or do they disappear after final payment?
          </p>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            <strong>Red flag:</strong> Defensive attitude. "That never happens." No clear answer about fixing issues.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Red Flags to Avoid</h2>
          
          <div class="space-y-4 my-8 text-gray-700 font-sans">
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="text-2xl text-gopro-orange">⚠️</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Demands large upfront payment.</strong> A reasonable deposit is 20-30%. Asking for 50% or more upfront before starting work is a red flag.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="text-2xl text-gopro-orange">⚠️</span>
              <div>
                <strong>No physical address or local presence.</strong> P.O. boxes and out-of-state phone numbers make it easy to disappear if there's a problem.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="text-2xl text-gopro-orange">⚠️</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Pressure to decide immediately.</strong> "This price is only good today" or "I have another client ready to book this slot." Good contractors give you time to think.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="text-2xl text-gopro-orange">⚠️</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Quote seems too good to be true.</strong> If one quote is 40% lower than others, they're either cutting corners, using cheap materials, or planning to upsell mid-job.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="text-2xl text-gopro-orange">⚠️</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Won't provide written contract.</strong> Everything should be in writing: scope, timeline, payment schedule, materials. Handshake deals end badly when there's a dispute.
              </div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
              <span class="text-2xl text-gopro-orange">⚠️</span>
              <div>
                <strong>Bad online reviews with no response.</strong> Every contractor gets a bad review eventually. What matters is how they respond. Ignoring complaints or getting defensive is a red flag.
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">What Actually Matters</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Experience in Your Type of Job</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            A contractor who's done 200 interior paint jobs knows how to work fast, clean, and handle surprises. A contractor who's done 10 paint jobs is still learning. Ask how many similar projects they've completed.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Communication</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Does the contractor return calls and texts promptly? Do they explain the process clearly? Good communication during the estimate tells you what communication will be like during the job.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Transparency</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Honest contractors tell you when something will cost extra, when timelines might shift, and what to realistically expect. If a contractor only says what you want to hear, they're selling, not advising.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Local Reputation</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            In <a href="/areas/kissimmee" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Kissimmee</a>, <a href="/areas/celebration" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Celebration</a>, <a href="/areas/st-cloud" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">St. Cloud</a>, and <a href="/areas/poinciana" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">Poinciana</a>, reputation spreads fast. Check Google reviews, ask neighbors, look at local Facebook groups. Contractors with consistent positive feedback over years are a safer bet than contractors with five 5-star reviews all posted the same week.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">Things That Don't Matter As Much As You Think</h2>
          
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Website Design</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            A slick website doesn't mean quality work. A basic website doesn't mean bad work. Judge contractors on experience and references, not how much they spent on web design.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">Being the Cheapest</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Lowest price often means corners cut. Fair pricing + experience + good communication is the sweet spot. Paying 10% more for a contractor who shows up on time and does it right is worth it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide mt-8">License Numbers (in Some Cases)</h3>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Florida doesn't require state licenses for painting, <a href="/services/drywall" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">drywall</a>, or <a href="/services/power-washing" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">power washing</a>. General contractors need licenses. Specialty trades vary. Don't assume "no license = bad contractor" for unlicensed trades. Insurance and experience matter more.
          </p>

          <h2 class="text-3xl font-display font-black uppercase mb-6 tracking-tight mt-12">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-lg text-gray-700 mb-6 font-sans">
            Hiring a home improvement contractor in Central Florida comes down to experience, communication, transparency, and local reputation. Ask questions, check references, get written estimates, and trust your gut. If something feels off during the estimate, it'll feel worse during the job.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-lg p-8 my-8">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-display font-bold uppercase mb-4 tracking-wide">Ready to Work with an Owner-Operated Contractor?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 font-sans mb-6">
              We're Go Pro Home Improvements—owner-operated, based in Central Florida since 2014. Painting, drywall, power washing, trim, TV mounting, and more. Free estimates, clear communication, and we stand behind every job.
            </p>
            <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4">
              <a href="/estimate" class="btn-primary">
                Get Free Estimate
              </a>
              <a href="tel:4072446873" class="btn-secondary">
                Call (407) 244-6873
              </a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-gray-200">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 font-sans">
              <a href="/guides" class="text-gopro-orange hover:underline">← Back to All Guides</a>
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTASection
    title="Need a Trustworthy Contractor?"
    description="Owner-operated. 15+ years serving Kissimmee, Orlando & Central Florida."
  />

  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
